John the Baptist described his own baptism as an oar, and the baptism of Jesus as a winnowing fan.  (Winnowing Oar - Wikipedia (https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Winnowing_Oar) )  I state it that way more for highlighting a parallel in Greek Mythology rather than clarity.  
For clarity, if you have the Holy Spirit, you are like wheat fit for grinding in the mill and made into bread for consumption.  If you are chaff you are garbage, fit only for the fire.  (Notice neither of these options actually hold the promise for eternal life.  Sorry.) How then, does Jesus operate as a Winnowing Fan?  Is it by presenting good teaching?  Some.  But if you just present good teaching, how does that separate the wheat from the chaff?  Instead, Jesus offers some good teaching, and some bad teaching, and you can tell what type of person you’re dealing with depending on their choices of teaching and their interpretations of the verses they have picked.  
Matthew 12:32 (https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Matthew%2012%3A32&version=NIV)Anyone who speaks a word against the** Son of Man** will be forgiven, but anyone who speaks against the **Holy** **Spirit** will not be forgiven, either in this age or in the age to come.  Luke 12:10 (https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Luke%2012%3A10&version=NIV)And everyone who speaks a word against the Son of Man will be forgiven, but anyone who blasphemes against the **Holy** **Spirit** will not be forgiven. 
Son of Man deserves a whole chapter for itself.  Let’s just give four hypotheses.  (1) Son of Man is any male human being except Adam.  (2) Son of Man also exclude Jesus, because he was born of God, not man.  (3)  Son of Man is Ezekiel, and Jesus is repeating the behavior of Ezekiel.  (4) Son of Man is Jesus, and not any other Man.  
If (1) then this somewhat conflicts with the 9th commandment, where we are advised not to say anything false against our neighbor.  It also somewhat conflicts with the Golden Rule, because we are to love our neighbor as ourselves.  However, you should keep in mind the 9th commandment, and the Golden Rule when testifying against your neighbor. (Consider John Rawls’ Veil of Ignorance).  (2) If this is the case then John-Gospel-Jesus is a different person than the Synoptic gospel Jesus, and it makes sense to criticize John-Gospel-Jesus (3) If this is the case, then it would make sense to criticize Ezekiel, and his teachings (especially Ezekiel 8–9, in my opinion) (4) In this case, it would make sense to criticize Jesus and every teaching of Jesus.  
But let’s put things together here.  John the Baptist tells us that Jesus will baptize us with fire and the holy spirit.  So it would make sense, I think, to carefully analyze the teachings of Jesus and see whether they are fit for the fire, or whether they come from the Holy Spirit.  Instead of letting yourself be baptized according to the winnowing fan, find a **tested** **stone**, a precious cornerstone for a sure foundation; the one who relies on it will never be stricken with panic.  (Isaiah 28:16)
My tested stone is (1) acknowledge the hypothesis;  any verse should be put into context and one should brainstorm for possibilities of meaning, and not reject a hypothesis on account that it seems stupid or evil, at this stage.  (2) test the hypothesis according to plausibility, or if plausibility is not available, use the “two witness” rule (Deuteronomy 17:6, 19:15) .  (3) test the hypothesis for “goodness”  That means, if a moral teaching appears, I test it against other moral teachings and apply a sort of informal rubric of “bad, good, better, best”.  

As an example, John’s gospel shows Jesus and his disciples take two fish and five loaves from a small boy.  The synoptics show Jesus and his disciples offer their own food.  In my opinion, taking the small boy’s food and making it miraculously increase is “good” but offering your own food and making it miraculously increase is “best”.  Others have argued, though, that the experience was a great opportunity for the boy to share his food, and be used to spread the message of Jesus.  
