1. Mark 12:36 (https://www.biblegateway.com/passage/?search=Mark%2012%3A36&version=NIV) David himself, speaking by the **Holy** **Spirit**, declared: “‘The Lord said to my Lord: “Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet.”’

This one is very confusing.  When David says “the Lord said to my Lord”, I think he seems to be experiencing a common problem symptomatic of several of the prophets.  He is receiving conflicting messages and beginning to confuse one for another.  Certainly, here, David is “by the Holy Spirit” there are two Lords here.  “the Lord” and “my Lord”.  David’s Lord, he would call “my Lord”, and David’s Lord has “The Holy Spirit.”  But the Lord who has “The Holy Spirit” is being told by “the Lord” to “Sit at my right hand until I put your enemies under your feet.”  The question, then is whether that’s appropriate in this age?  
Should the Lord that has the Holy Spirit sit at the right hand of the Lord that promises “I will put your enemies under your feet”?  Perhaps.  I would certainly rather that our armies fight against evil rather than for it.  But I think there is still a big difference between “the Lord” whose spirit is to make your enemies a footstool, and “David’s Lord” whose spirit is Holy.  The first offers the authority to kill in it’s name.  The Holy Spirit does not offer that authority.  The enemies made into a footstool for the Holy Spirit are still enemies of the Holy Spirit.  

